---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { loadData } from '../lib/data';
import { formatDate, href, site } from '../lib/site';

const { parties, topics, pledges } = await loadData();
const sources = [
	{ name: 'Victorian Electoral Commission – registered political parties', url: 'https://vec.vic.gov.au/candidates-and-parties/registered-political-parties/currently-registered-parties' },
	{ name: 'Victorian Parliamentary Budget Office – 2026 Election Commitment Tracker', url: 'https://pbo.vic.gov.au/2026_election_commitment_tracker' },
	{ name: 'Victorian Parliamentary Budget Office – policy costings and advice', url: 'https://pbo.vic.gov.au/policy_costings_and_advice' },
	{ name: 'ABC News – 2026 election policy promise tracker', url: 'https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-11-04/victoria-2026-election-policy-promise-tracker/107247200' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="About – how this site works" description="Method, sources and limitations of the Victorian Election 2026 pledge comparison site.">
	<h1>About this site</h1>
	<p class="lead">
		This site collects and summarises what political parties say they will do if elected at the 2026 Victorian
		state election. It is a reference tool, not a campaign: there are no scores, rankings or recommendations.
	</p>

	<h2>What is included</h2>
	<p>
		Eight parties are covered: the four parties with seats in the current parliament from the major blocs
		(Labor, Liberal, Nationals and Greens), plus One Nation, Legalise Cannabis, Animal Justice and the
		Libertarian Party. The Liberal Party and The Nationals contest on a joint platform; joint pledges are
		recorded once and marked as shared. The Nationals also publish separate regional policies, which are
		listed on their own page.
	</p>
	<p>
		There are currently <strong>{pledges.length}</strong> pledges across <strong>{topics.length}</strong> topics.
		Each entry has a short neutral summary, a verbatim excerpt where available, a costing when the party or an
		independent body has published one, and a direct link to the source.
	</p>

	<h2>Method</h2>
	<ol>
		<li>We read each party's official election platform or policy pages, and policy documents such as PDF booklets where published.</li>
		<li>We record forward-looking commitments — what the party says it will do. Statements about past achievements are generally excluded, but some platforms mix the two.</li>
		<li>Summaries use the party's own description, stripped of rhetoric. Where a figure is a party estimate, the summary says so.</li>
		<li>Each entry is assigned one topic. A pledge that covers several areas appears once, under the closest topic.</li>
		<li>Costings are included only when the source states one. “Not stated in the source” means we found no published figure; it does not mean the policy has no cost.</li>
		<li>Every source link is checked, and the date it was checked is shown on each entry. Party pages can change during a campaign.</li>
	</ol>

	<h2>Limitations</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Parties announce policies throughout the campaign.</strong> The data was last checked on {formatDate(site.dataLastVerified)}. Treat this as a snapshot.</li>
		<li><strong>Coverage is not equal.</strong> Legalise Cannabis has no formal platform, so its pledges come from news posts, MPs' candidate pages and its how-to-vote card. Smaller parties often describe positions rather than costed programs.</li>
		<li><strong>Not all pledges are included.</strong> We focus on significant, specific commitments and skip local announcements for individual schools or streets.</li>
		<li><strong>Costings are not audited here.</strong> Where available, independent Parliamentary Budget Office figures are linked. A party's own cost estimate is labelled as its own.</li>
		<li><strong>No endorsement.</strong> Inclusion is not approval. Summaries describe what parties say; they do not assess whether a pledge is achievable, desirable or truthful. Check the source and independent analysis before voting.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Sources and cross-checks</h2>
	<ul>
		{sources.map((s) => <li><a href={s.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{s.name} ↗</a></li>)}
	</ul>
	<p>The direct link to each party's manifesto or policy page is on the <a href={href('')}>home page</a> and each party page.</p>
	<p>The social-sharing banner uses cropped candidate portraits; see each candidate page for photo credits and licences.</p>

	<h2>Corrections</h2>
	<p>
		If a summary is wrong, a source link is broken or a pledge is missing, please <a href={`mailto:${site.contactEmail}`}>email us</a>.
		We will correct errors and note material changes.
	</p>

	<h2>Electoral authorisation</h2>
	<p>
		{site.authorisation} This site is non-partisan and educational, and does not endorse any party.
		The Victorian Electoral Commission advises that authorisation statements normally include a full name
		and street address; see its <a href="https://vec.vic.gov.au/candidates-and-parties/become-a-state-election-candidate/authorisation" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">authorisation guidance ↗</a>.
	</p>

	<h2>Data freshness</h2>
	<p>Party records were last verified {formatDate(site.dataLastVerified)} · Election day {formatDate(site.electionDate)}.</p>
</BaseLayout>
